WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.350
Groups and service accounts each get
one recorded decision per object.

2
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.214
For groups, the AGDLP model
doesn't map one to one,

3
00:00:08.214 --> 00:00:11.087
because Entra ID has no group scope.

4
00:00:11.087 --> 00:00:14.675
So every AD security group
gets one of five decisions.

5
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:17.542
Used only for on-premises resource ACLs:

6
00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:21.800
keep it in AD and don't sync it,
since a cloud copy adds nothing.

7
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.263
Used for both on-premises
and cloud: synchronize it,

8
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:29.138
with AD as the source of
authority during the transition.

9
00:00:29.138 --> 00:00:32.974
Used only for cloud resources:
convert it to cloud-only.

10
00:00:32.974 --> 00:00:37.787
Membership follows attributes exactly:
convert it to a dynamic group.

11
00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:41.038
No members or no assignments: retire it.

12
00:00:41.038 --> 00:00:45.231
Where a cloud-only group still has
to guard a Kerberos application,

13
00:00:45.231 --> 00:00:48.738
Cloud Sync provisions it back
to AD as a universal group.

14
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:51.699
Nesting works for fewer
things in Entra ID.

15
00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:56.159
Conditional Access follows nested
members, but group-based application

16
00:00:56.159 --> 00:01:00.325
assignment and group-based licensing
don't cascade to nested groups.

17
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:04.863
So flatten deep AGDLP chains to
direct membership or a dynamic rule,

18
00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:07.700
and record the mapping
for the audit trail.

19
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.800
For service accounts, the rule
is to eliminate stored secrets.

20
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.650
A traditional account that calls Microsoft
365 or other Entra-protected APIs becomes

21
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.981
an application registration
with a certificate,

22
00:01:20.981 --> 00:01:24.962
or a federated credential where
the host has an OIDC issuer.

23
00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:27.635
One whose workload runs
on an Azure resource

24
00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:30.813
becomes a managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

25
00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:34.581
On-premises Kerberos workloads,
and standalone MSAs,

26
00:01:34.581 --> 00:01:37.237
go to a group managed service account.

27
00:01:37.237 --> 00:01:42.462
A gMSA whose workload moves to the cloud
goes to workload identity federation.

28
00:01:42.462 --> 00:01:46.424
A federated credential has
three working parts: the issuer,

29
00:01:46.424 --> 00:01:50.951
a subject that must match exactly
with no wildcards, and an audience.

30
00:01:50.951 --> 00:01:53.503
An application holds at most twenty,

31
00:01:53.503 --> 00:01:58.012
and a wrong subject fails only at
token exchange, so test each one.

32
00:01:58.012 --> 00:02:03.000
Every application gets two human owners,
and no client secrets in production.
